---
import Docs from '../../layouts/Docs.astro';
import ModelSelectorDemo from '../../components/ModelSelectorDemo';
import CodeBlock from '../../components/CodeBlock.astro';
---

<Docs title="Model Selector" description="Pick a chat model by its logo, speed, quality, and cost.">
  <h1 class="docs-title">Model Selector</h1>
  <p class="docs-lead">
    A searchable model picker for chat. The button shows the model's logo and name. The list has a search field and
    shows each model with its logo, a short description, and three colored meters for speed, quality, and cost, rated
    against the other models in the list.
  </p>
  <div class="mt-8">
    <ModelSelectorDemo client:load />
  </div>
  <section class="prose-section">
    <h2 class="section-title">Usage</h2>
    <CodeBlock
      lang="tsx"
      title="Model Selector usage"
      code={`import { ModelSelector } from "@tulipfarm/sprout/model-selector";

const models = [
  { id: "opus", label: "Claude Opus 5.5", description: "Hardest problems", brand: "claude", speed: 45, quality: 92, cost: 75 },
  { id: "gpt", label: "GPT-5.5", description: "Writing and analysis", brand: "openai", speed: 70, quality: 90, cost: 30 },
  { id: "deepseek", label: "DeepSeek V4", description: "Cheap and capable", brand: "deepseek", speed: 60, quality: 82, cost: 1.1 },
];

const [model, setModel] = useState("opus");

<ModelSelector models={models} value={model} onValueChange={setModel} />`}
    />
    <p>
      <code>brand</code> draws the vendor logo from Brand Icon. Pass <code>icon</code> for any other mark; with neither,
      the first letter of <code>label</code> stands in. <code>side</code> and <code>align</code> place the list, and{' '}
      <code>label</code> (default <code>Model</code>) names the button and heads the first column.
    </p>
  </section>
  <section class="prose-section">
    <h2 class="section-title">Speed, quality, and cost</h2>
    <p>
      Pass raw numbers in any unit, such as tokens per second for <code>speed</code>, an eval score for{' '}
      <code>quality</code>, and dollars per million tokens for <code>cost</code>. Higher means faster, better, or
      pricier. Each meter rates the model from 1 to 5 between the lowest number in the list (1) and the highest (5), so
      prices never show. When every model has the same number, they all get 3.
    </p>
    <p>
      Hover a column heading to see what it measures and what one to five bars mean, from slowest to fastest, weakest to
      strongest, or cheapest to priciest.
    </p>
    <p>
      A model missing a number shows a dash for it. When no model has numbers, the meters and their headings go away.{' '}
      <code>rateModels(models)</code> returns the same ratings if you need them elsewhere.
    </p>
  </section>
  <section class="prose-section">
    <h2 class="section-title">In Chat Input</h2>
    <p>
      Chat Input uses Model Selector for its <code>models</code> picker. Give its models <code>speed</code>,{' '}
      <code>quality</code>, and <code>cost</code> to show the meters there too. Pass <code>render</code> to use your own
      button, as Chat Input does with <code>InputGroupButton</code>.
    </p>
  </section>
  <section class="prose-section">
    <h2 class="section-title">Motion</h2>
    <p>
      The list grows from the button and the chevron turns over. On the highlighted and chosen rows the meters take
      their full color, filling left to right. With reduced motion, the list only fades and the meters change at once.
    </p>
  </section>
  <section class="prose-section">
    <h2 class="section-title">Accessibility</h2>
    <p>
      The button and list are a Base UI combobox with its search field inside the list. Opening moves focus to the
      search; typing filters by name, description, or vendor, arrow keys move, Enter picks, and Escape closes and
      returns focus to the button. The button reads “Model: Claude Opus 5.5”. Each option reads its name, description,
      and ratings, such as “Speed 1 of 5, slowest. Quality 5 of 5, strongest. Cost 5 of 5, priciest.” The column
      headings and their hover tooltips are hidden from screen readers, since each option names its own ratings. In
      forced colors, meters draw in system colors.
    </p>
  </section>
  <section class="prose-section">
    <h2 class="section-title">Theme tokens</h2>
    <p>
      Set <code>--sprout-model-selector-speed</code>, <code>--sprout-model-selector-quality</code>, and{' '}
      <code>--sprout-model-selector-cost</code> on <code>:root</code> to recolor the meters, since the list renders in a
      portal. They default to <code>--sprout-green</code>, <code>--sprout-blue</code>, and <code>--sprout-orange</code>.
      Other rows show those colors at 40%. The list uses <code>--sprout-surface</code>, <code>--sprout-line</code>, and{' '}
      <code>--sprout-radius-panel</code>, like Select.
    </p>
  </section>
</Docs>
